Doc 0 — 2026.01.16.696667v3.full.pdf
Medea: An AI agent for therapeutic reasoning across biological contexts Pengwei Sui1,∗, Michelle M. Li1,2,∗,‡, Brenton P. Munson3,4, Shanghua Gao1, Wanxiang Shen1, Valentina Giunchiglia1,5,6, Andrew Shen1, Yepeng Huang1, Zhenglun Kong1, Katherine Licon3, Trey Ideker3,7,8,9,‡, and Marinka Zitnik1,2,10,11,12,‡ 1Department of Biomedical Informatics, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, USA 2The Ivan and Francesca Berkowitz Family Living Laboratory Collaboration at Harvard Medical School and Clalit Research Institute, Boston, MA, USA 3Department of Medicine, University of California, San Diego, La Jolla, CA, USA 4Department of Bioengineering, University of California, San Diego, La Jolla, CA, USA 5Department of Brain Sciences, Imperial College London, London, UK 6Centre for Neuroimaging Sciences, King’s College London, London, UK 7Big Data Institute, University of Oxford, Oxford, UK 8Department of Computer Science and Engineering, University of California, San Diego, La Jolla, CA, USA 9Moores Cancer Center, University of California, San Diego, San Diego, CA, USA 10Kempner Institute for the Study of Natural and Artificial Intelligence, Harvard University, Allston, MA, USA 11Broad Institute of MIT and Harvard, Cambridge, MA, USA 12Harvard Data Science Initiative, Cambridge, MA, USA ∗Equal contribution. ‡Correspondence: michelleli@g.harvard.edu, trey.ideker@bdi.ox.ac.uk, marinka@hms.harvard.edu Medea is at https://medea.openscientist.ai Medea code and benchmarks are at https://github.com/mims-harvard/Medea Abstract Therapeutic hypotheses can transfer across diseases but their relevance depends on biologi- cal context. The same target, perturbation, or treatment can produce different effects across cell types, disease states, genetic backgrounds, and patients. Therapeutic reasoning therefore requires methods that preserve context, test when evidence supports transfer, and identify where context-specific effects limit it. Although AI agents can perform therapeutic analyses, existing systems often fail to preserve biological context over long workflows, verify inter- mediate computational steps, or reconcile conflicting evidence across datasets and literature. Here, we present Medea, an AI agent for therapeutic reasoning  [...] Conclusion The overall immune profile suggests a dysfunctional tumormicroenvironment dominated by exhausted T cells, which may limit the effectiveness of PD-L1 inhibition despite the favorableTMB. Based on the existed studies , the balance between the potentially immunogenic high TMB and the apparent defects in cellular immunity creates a complex scenario that predisposes the patient toward reduced responsiveness to atezolizumab … Medea Humanbase COMPASS Semantic Scholar OpenAlex Semantic Scholar OpenAlex COMPASS dExperiment instruction A Experiment instruction B 1.Apply COMPASS to predict the patient's likelihood of responding to Atezolizumab and extract the top five most relevant immune-related concepts 2.Combine these biomarker measureme

================================== Ai Message ==================================

1. [✓] Analyzed the provided document excerpt for mentions of tuberculosis (TB) or Mycobacterium tuberculosis (M. tuberculosis).  
2. [✓] Determined that the document focuses on cancer immunotherapy, specifically checkpoint inhibitors like PD-L1 and atezolizumab, and discusses tumor microenvironment, T cells, and immune response.  
3. [✓] Confirmed no mention of TB or M. tuberculosis in the excerpt, making the document unrelated to TB.  

